{"conjecture": "Long-running software systems accumulate an unobservable dependency graph between internal state variables that no individual change can detect, because each modification is locally correct against the immediately prior state but blind to the transitive closure of state couplings it creates. Once this hidden coupling density exceeds a critical threshold, the cost of any change acquires a catastrophic risk premium proportional to the number of unobservable state paths it might perturb, making the system effectively unchangeable even though every prior change was individually justified. This premium is structurally invisible to testing because tests only verify observed state, not the exponentially larger space of unobserved state interactions."}
